{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"═══ LIVE EVIDENCE: the declarations ARE honoured. This is the inverse of the original probe. ═══\n\nA real `rebar review-code --base HEAD~1 --head HEAD` (attested source) on commit 2e17171e5c, from\nthe worktree build carrying the fix, with `REBAR_USAGE_LOG` set and a **DISCRIMINATING** class table.\n\n**THE TABLE MATTERS, and getting it wrong is why the original probe was ambiguous.** With no\n`model_classes` configured, `frontier` resolves to the built-in `claude-opus-4-8` — which IS\n`cfg.model` — so a frontier-declaring step runs on opus whether or not the declaration is honoured.\nThis run therefore made all three classes differ from `cfg.model`:\n    cfg.model  = claude-opus-4-8      (the value a dropped declaration would fall through to)\n    frontier   = claude-sonnet-4-6\n    standard   = claude-haiku-4-5\nSo ANY opus row would prove a dropped declaration. There are none.\n\n    step      op                                           model_class  model\n    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n    base      code-review-base                             frontier     anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6\n    round_a   code-review-docs                             frontier     anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6\n    round_a   code-review-tests                            frontier     anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6\n    round_a   code-review-deletion-impact                  frontier     anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6\n    round_a   code-review-project-review-phase-boundaries   frontier     anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6\n    verify    code-review-verify                           standard     anthropic:claude-haiku-4-5\n    coach     code-review-coach                            standard     anthropic:claude-haiku-4-5\n\n**SEVEN CALLS, ZERO ON `cfg.model`.** Compare the ticket's original table: nine calls, ALL on\n`cfg.model`, including `code-review-verify`. `verify` and `coach` are the two steps that discriminate\n(they declare `standard`), and both now run on the standard class. `base` covers the prompt-step\nshape (step-level `model:`) and `round_a` covers the batch shape (`batch.model_ladder[0]`), so both\ncode paths are demonstrated live, not just offline.\n\n**AND EVERY ROW CARRIES `step` + `model_class` — Step 0 working in a real run.** Neither field\nexisted before this ticket. Note what the `round_a` rows show about the plan's premise: `op` is the\nOVERLAY prompt (`code-review-docs`, …), not the `batch.prompt` finder — so the pass a call belongs to\nis recoverable ONLY from `step`. That is the attribution the ticket exists to add, and it is what\nmakes any future per-pass claim in this epic checkable.\n\n**GATE + CI STATE.** Gerrit **1178** reached `LLM-Review +1` (PASS) and `Verified +1` with 0\nunresolved comments on patch set 1. NOT submitted — the stack lands only when epic 061c closes.\nFull unit suite: **6419 passed, 19 skipped**. (One ERROR in that run was a harness guard —\n`Test moved the repo HEAD (44d9250eab -> 2e17171e5c)` — tripped by my own mid-run commit, not a\nregression: `tests/unit/grounding/test_coverage_reconstruction.py` re-run against a stable HEAD is\n5 passed. Recording it because an unexplained ERROR in a log is exactly what a later reader would\nhave to re-litigate.)\n\n**MY OWN CHANGE WENT THROUGH `rebar review-code` AND IT FOUND A REAL TEST GAP — fixed in patch set 2.**\nVerdict PASS, 0 blocking, 3 advisory:\n 1. *(medium, tests — VALID, FIXED)* the usage-log `model_class` assertion covered `base`/`verify`/\n    `coach` but NOT the batch steps, leaving the `_run_batch` `step_identity` binding unguarded; and\n    it keyed a dict by step id, so with several rows per batch pass it asserted only whichever row\n    was last. Now asserts EVERY row of all five steps. Mutation-verified: replacing the batch token\n    with `None` fails with `step 'round_a' (op 'code-review-security') recorded model_class None,\n    expected 'frontier'`. The reviewer was right and the guard was weaker than I thought.\n 2. *(info, edge-cases — behaviour KEPT, rationale documented)* `_effective_config` treats an empty\n    requested model as \"no override\" instead of raising. Kept deliberately: substituting an empty\n    model would build a provider session for nothing and fail deep in the SDK, while falling back to\n    the run-wide floor is exactly what a step declaring no `model:` already does. Docstring now says\n    so rather than leaving it to be re-derived.\n 3. *(medium, correctness — REAL, PRE-EXISTING, NOT SILENTLY DISMISSED)* a FAILED LLM call writes no\n    usage row at all, because `record()` sits after `run()`'s try/except and `interpret_failure`\n    always re-raises — so failed calls are invisible to per-step attribution. Verified, and verified\n    to be pre-existing (`record()` has always sat there; this ticket only added fields to rows that\n    are written). Failure spend is not lost — `usage_log.failure_usage()` is attached as\n    `exc.diagnostic` in `structured_run.py` and reaches the gate-error sidecar — but it does NOT\n    reach the usage log, so a spend total read from the JSONL under-reports failed calls. Filing it\n    as its own ticket rather than widening this one or calling it out of scope."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785622762638229001,"uuid":"13c05d6f-8776-46ae-b5ae-af07e725de69"}